Backport of the upstream fix that initializes `nonnull` in build_x_typeid.

In gcc 2.95.2, build_x_typeid (gcc/cp/rtti.c) passes the address of the local
`int nonnull` to resolves_to_fixed_type_p and then reads it, but
fixed_type_or_null (gcc/cp/class.c) returns without setting it when it cannot
prove the pointer non-null. Whether `typeid (*p)` keeps its null check (the call
to __throw_bad_typeid) then depends on what that stack slot held. The Sony
cc1plus (built natively for the R5900) reads 0 there and keeps the check, ours
(built for i386) reads a pointer and drops it.

Upstream fixed it twice, after the 2.95 branch (gcc/cp/ChangeLog):
  1999-10-11  Jason Merrill   class.c (fixed_type_or_null): Always set *nonnull.
  2000-01-17  Mark Mitchell   rtti.c (build_x_typeid): Initialize NONNULL.
This patch is the second one, the line gcc 3.0 has. Both give nonnull = 0 when
the pointer is not proven non-null: the check stays, as in the original.

It changes only `typeid (*p)` on polymorphic types. Verified on what__C9exception
of top.so (libgcc exception.o) and against the kit's libgcc.a, built by the
real compiler. bn never uses typeid: its objects do not change.

Apply it after the three Sony patches (docker/Dockerfile.sonygcc does).
Details: docs/compiler.md. tools/check_toolchain.py pins it by sha256.

--- a/gcc/cp/rtti.c
+++ b/gcc/cp/rtti.c
@@ -262,7 +262,7 @@
 {
   tree cond = NULL_TREE;
   tree type;
-  int nonnull;
+  int nonnull = 0;

   if (! flag_rtti)
     {
